# ==================================================================================================
# LINE ENDINGS ARE LOAD-BEARING IN THIS REPO
# ==================================================================================================
#
# This repo had no .gitattributes and the machines building it run `core.autocrlf=true`, so a
# Windows clone rewrote every text file to CRLF on checkout while the stored blobs stayed LF.
#
# That is not cosmetic here. Several suites are SOURCE-TEXT tests: they `readFileSync` a component
# and assert that it contains an exact string, newlines included. `settleAmountIsOutstanding.test.ts`
# is the one that caught this -- it asserts on
#
#     'selectCashSettleableOrders(\n      orders,\n      requestedOrderIds,\n      payable,\n    )'
#
# and reads the file raw, with no newline normalisation. On a CRLF checkout every such assertion
# fails, so the suite went red 2/15 for anyone with a fresh clone while passing for whoever happened
# to have LF on disk. A test that depends on the checkout that produced it is not a test.
#
# Fixing it in the tests would mean teaching every source-text suite to normalise before matching,
# and remembering to do so in the next one. Fixing it here fixes it once, for every reader of the
# tree -- git, jest, Metro and the editor all see the same bytes.
#
# `eol=lf` is stated explicitly rather than relying on `text=auto` alone: `text=auto` normalises
# what is STORED, and would still hand a CRLF working tree to anyone with core.autocrlf=true. The
# whole problem is in the working tree.
# ==================================================================================================

# Every text file: LF in the repository AND in the working tree, whatever core.autocrlf says.
# `text=auto` keeps git's binary detection, so a file it judges binary is left alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE PLACE CRLF IS CORRECT
#
# A Windows batch file is parsed by cmd.exe, which is not reliably tolerant of bare LF -- a `goto`
# past a label can misread. gradlew.bat is how the Android build is invoked on these machines, so it
# keeps CRLF in the working tree. The blob stays LF, which is what it already is.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bat text eol=crlf

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NEVER NORMALISE, NEVER DIFF
#
# Marked explicitly rather than left to `text=auto`, because the costly case is a binary that LOOKS
# textual. `android/app/src/main/assets/index.android.bundle` is exactly that: 1.5 MB of generated
# JavaScript with no NUL byte, so `text=auto` would call it text and rewrite it. The two bundles
# under releases/ ARE binary (Hermes bytecode). Both are build outputs; normalising either is churn
# at best and corruption at worst.
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.bundle binary
*.jar     binary
*.aar     binary
*.keystore binary
*.apk     binary
*.aab     binary
*.zip     binary
*.so      binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.jpeg    binary
*.gif     binary
*.webp    binary
*.ico     binary
*.ttf     binary
*.otf     binary
